\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Finite-field partition functions}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{What takes the place of a Gaussian partition function when field values lie
in a finite field? Let #{p} be an odd prime and let #{\zeta_p^{t}=\exp(2\pi \ui t/p)}
for the representative of #{t\in\mathbb F_p} in #{\{0,\ldots,p-1\}}. For a
symmetric #{n\times n} matrix #{Q} over #{\mathbb F_p}, define
##{Z_p(Q)=\sum_{x\in\mathbb F_p^n}\zeta_p^{x^TQx}.}
The exponent is the full quadratic form #{x^TQx}, not half of it. The sum
is a complex number. It is not a probability. The bilinear radical
#{\operatorname{rad}(Q)} is the set of vectors #{v} with #{x^TQv=0} for
every #{x}. Since #{p} is odd, this radical is the kernel of #{Q}.}

\p{Write #{\bigl(\frac{a}{p}\bigr)} for the Legendre symbol, and
##{g=\sum_{t\in\mathbb F_p}\zeta_p^{t^2}.}
If #{\det Q\neq0} in #{\mathbb F_p}, then
##{Z_p(Q)=\Bigl(\frac{\det Q}{p}\Bigr)g^n,\qquad g^2=\Bigl(\frac{-1}{p}\Bigr)p.}
In particular, when #{n} is divisible by #{4},
##{Z_p(Q)=\Bigl(\frac{\det Q}{p}\Bigr)p^{n/2},}
an ordinary integer. If a coordinate split has #{Q_{UU}} invertible, with
Schur complement #{S} and #{B=-Q_{UU}^{-1}Q_{UR}} as in the
[Wick state](fdip-008N), then for every retained vector
##{\sum_{x_U}\zeta_p^{x^TQx}=Z_p(Q_{UU})\,\zeta_p^{x_R^TSx_R}.}
The same block factorization gives #{\det Q=\det Q_{UU}\det S}, so the
Legendre symbol of the determinant factors as a product.}

\proof{
\p{For #{a\in\mathbb F_p}, put #{g(a)=\sum_t\zeta_p^{at^2}}. Then #{g(0)=p}.
The value #{g(a)} depends only on the square class of #{a}: if #{a=c^2b}
with #{c\neq0}, the substitution #{t=c^{-1}s} gives #{g(a)=g(b)}.
There are #{(p-1)/2} nonzero squares and the same number of nonsquares.
Summing the nontrivial additive character,
##{\sum_{a\neq0}g(a)=\sum_t\sum_{a\neq0}\zeta_p^{at^2}=(p-1)+(p-1)(-1)=0,}
because the inner sum is #{p-1} at #{t=0} and #{-1} for #{t\neq0}.
Hence #{g(\nu)=-g} for any nonsquare #{\nu}, which is
#{g(a)=\bigl(\frac{a}{p}\bigr)g} for #{a\neq0}.}

\p{The change of variables #{u=x-y}, #{v=x+y} is bijective on
#{\mathbb F_p^2} because #{2} is invertible:
#{x=2^{-1}(u+v)} and #{y=2^{-1}(v-u)}. It turns the difference of squares
into a product, so
##{\sum_{x,y}\zeta_p^{x^2-y^2}=\sum_{u,v}\zeta_p^{uv}=p.}
The left side is #{g\cdot g(-1)=\bigl(\frac{-1}{p}\bigr)g^2}. Therefore
#{g^2=\bigl(\frac{-1}{p}\bigr)p}.}

\p{Over a field of characteristic not #{2}, a symmetric matrix is congruent
to a diagonal matrix. If #{Q=0} it is already diagonal. Otherwise some
quadratic value is nonzero: if every #{v^TQv} vanished, the polarization
identity
##{(x+y)^TQ(x+y)-x^TQx-y^TQy=2x^TQy}
would force every bilinear value to vanish, hence #{Q=0}. Take such a
vector first in a basis, and replace each later basis vector #{w} by
#{w-(v^TQw)(v^TQv)^{-1}v}. The change is invertible, those vectors are
orthogonal to #{v}, and the form splits off the line through #{v}.
Induction diagonalizes the complement.}

\p{If #{A} is invertible and #{R=A^TQA}, then #{y=A^{-1}x} runs through
#{\mathbb F_p^n} as #{x} does, and #{x^TQx=y^TRy}. Thus #{Z_p(Q)=Z_p(R)}.
Also #{\det R=(\det A)^2\det Q}, so the two matrices have the same Legendre
symbol. On a nondegenerate diagonal matrix
#{\operatorname{diag}(a_1,\ldots,a_n)} the sum factors as
#{\prod_ig(a_i)=\bigl(\frac{\det R}{p}\bigr)g^n}. This is the stated
formula. When #{4} divides #{n}, the exponent #{n/2} is even, so
##{g^n=(g^2)^{n/2}=\Bigl(\frac{-1}{p}\Bigr)^{n/2}p^{n/2}=p^{n/2}.}}

\p{For the elimination, set #{z=x_U-Bx_R}. As #{x_U} runs through the
eliminated space, so does #{z}. Expanding the symmetric form gives the
polynomial identity
##{x^TQx=z^TQ_{UU}z+x_R^TSx_R,}
the same cancellation used for the quadratic completion in the Wick state,
applied to the full form rather than to half of it. Additivity of #{\zeta_p}
factors the sum.}

\p{Write #{Q} in retained and eliminated blocks and factor it as in the
Wick state, with unitriangular factors of determinant #{1} and a
block-diagonal factor #{\operatorname{diag}(S,Q_{UU})}. The determinant
multiplies, so #{\det Q=\det Q_{UU}\det S}.}
}

\p{The identity #{\det Q=\det Q_{UU}\det S} is the finite-field form of the
block factorization used for the [quadratic center characters](fdip-0044).
It does not prove that rational character identity.}

\p{For a symmetric #{n\times n} matrix over #{\mathbb F_p}, define the
signed discriminant
#{d_{\pm}(Q)=(-1)^{\frac{n(n-1)}{2}}\det Q} in #{\mathbb F_p}. If
#{\det Q\neq 0}, then
##{Z_p(Q)=\Bigl(\frac{d_{\pm}(Q)}{p}\Bigr)g^{\varepsilon}p^{m},}
where #{n=2m+\varepsilon} and #{\varepsilon} is #{0} or #{1}. When #{4}
divides #{n}, one has #{\varepsilon=0} and #{d_{\pm}(Q)=\det Q}, so this
is the power stated above.}
\proof{
\p{The nondegenerate evaluation gives
#{Z_p(Q)=\bigl(\frac{\det Q}{p}\bigr)g^n}, and
#{g^2=\bigl(\frac{-1}{p}\bigr)p}. Hence
#{g^n=g^{\varepsilon}(g^2)^m
=g^{\varepsilon}\bigl(\frac{-1}{p}\bigr)^m p^m}, and
##{Z_p(Q)=\Bigl(\frac{(-1)^m\det Q}{p}\Bigr)g^{\varepsilon}p^{m}.}
The exponent #{m} has the same parity as #{\frac{n(n-1)}{2}}. If
#{\varepsilon=0}, then #{n=2m} and
#{\frac{n(n-1)}{2}=m(2m-1)}; the factor #{2m-1} is odd. If
#{\varepsilon=1}, then #{n=2m+1} and
#{\frac{n(n-1)}{2}=m(2m+1)}; the factor #{2m+1} is odd. In both cases
#{(-1)^m=(-1)^{\frac{n(n-1)}{2}}}, so the symbol is that of #{d_{\pm}(Q)}.
When #{4} divides #{n}, the integer #{m} is even, and the sign is #{+1}.}
}

\p{Now suppose #{\det Q=0}, and let #{r} be the rank of #{Q}. Then
#{\dim\operatorname{rad}(Q)=n-r}. The same diagonalization, which does not
require a nonzero determinant, produces an invertible change of variables to
#{\operatorname{diag}(a_1,\ldots,a_r,0,\ldots,0)} with every #{a_i\neq 0}.
In the new coordinates the last #{n-r} basis vectors span the radical, and
#{Q'} represents the nondegenerate form induced on the quotient by that
radical. Then
##{Z_p(Q)=p^{\dim\operatorname{rad}(Q)}Z_p(Q')=p^{n-r}Z_p(Q'),}
where #{Q'=\operatorname{diag}(a_1,\ldots,a_r)}. If #{r=0}, the radical is
the whole domain, the original form is zero, and #{Z_p(Q)=p^n}. If
#{r\ge 1}, the signed evaluation on the quotient form #{Q'} gives
##{Z_p(Q)=p^{n-r}\Bigl(\frac{d_{\pm}(Q')}{p}\Bigr)g^{\varepsilon'}p^{m'},}
with #{r=2m'+\varepsilon'} and #{\varepsilon'} equal to #{0} or #{1}.
Here #{d_{\pm}(Q)=0}. That zero discriminant belongs to the degenerate
form; the identities below do not drop it.}
\proof{
\p{The diagonalization already proved for a form that is not identically
zero splits off a line whenever some quadratic value is nonzero, and it
stops when every remaining value is zero. On that diagonal matrix the
equation #{Qv=0} reads #{a_iv_i=0} for each #{i\le r}, so those
coefficients vanish and the zero block is the kernel. Since #{p} is odd,
that kernel is #{\operatorname{rad}(Q)}, and #{Q'} is the form on the
quotient by the radical. An invertible change
of variables preserves the partition
function, by the same substitution used in the nondegenerate case. On the
diagonal matrix the sum factors as a product of one-variable sums. Each
nonzero entry #{a_i} contributes #{g(a_i)}, and each zero entry contributes
#{\sum_t\zeta_p^{0}=p}. There are #{n-r} zero entries, so the power of #{p} is
#{p^{n-r}}, and the product of the #{g(a_i)} is #{Z_p(Q')}. The
nondegenerate evaluation and the signed formula apply to #{Q'}.}

\p{The symbol #{\bigl(\frac{d_{\pm}(Q')}{p}\bigr)} does not depend on the
choice of splitting. Another invertible substitution multiplies
#{\prod_ia_i} by a square, so it preserves the Legendre symbol of that
product. The sign #{\,(-1)^{\frac{r(r-1)}{2}}} depends only on the rank, and the
rank is unchanged by an invertible substitution. If #{r=0}, there is no
diagonal product: every one of the #{p^n} vectors contributes #{\zeta_p^{0}=1}.}
}

\p{Suppose a coordinate split has #{Q_{UU}} invertible, with Schur
complement #{S}, and write #{n_U} and #{n_R} for the two block sizes. Then
#{Z_p(Q)=Z_p(Q_{UU})Z_p(S)}, and
#{d_{\pm}(Q)=(-1)^{n_Un_R}d_{\pm}(Q_{UU})d_{\pm}(S)}.
The sign is #{+1} when #{n_U} or #{n_R} is even. Both identities use
invertibility of #{Q_{UU}} only. They do not require #{\det Q\neq 0}.
When the retained form is degenerate, #{d_{\pm}(S)=0} and therefore
#{d_{\pm}(Q)=0}: the product includes that zero discriminant.}
\proof{
\p{Summing the elimination identity over the retained vectors multiplies
the two partition functions. For the signed discriminants, the exponent
identity
##{\frac{n(n-1)}{2}
=\frac{n_U(n_U-1)}{2}+\frac{n_R(n_R-1)}{2}+n_Un_R}
holds for #{n=n_U+n_R}, and the block factorization gives
#{\det Q=\det Q_{UU}\det S}. Multiplying the signs produces the factor
#{(-1)^{n_Un_R}}.}
}

\p{On the massive four-cycle, #{n=4} and #{\det Q=45}, so
#{d_{\pm}(Q)=\det Q}, because the exponent #{\frac{4\cdot 3}{2}} equals
#{6}. For an odd prime that does not divide #{45},
#{Z_p(Q)=\bigl(\frac{45}{p}\bigr)p^2}. Since #{45=9\cdot 5} and #{9} is a
square, #{\bigl(\frac{9}{p}\bigr)=1}, and the value equals
#{\bigl(\frac{5}{p}\bigr)p^2}.}

\p{Characteristic #{2} is outside the argument, because both the
polarization step and the change of variables divide by #{2}. The kernel
dimension #{n-r} records the rank drop of #{Q} itself.
[Different domains](fdip-000W) separate the primes at which a chosen
elimination block stays invertible from the primes at which a trace ratio
stays regular; those loci are not this kernel. This section gives no
Euler product and no comparison with the real normalizer.}

\p{Remark. The identity #{\det Q=\det Q_{UU}\det S} is the
factorization of the action on the top exterior power. The Grassmann
form of that reading is the remark in \ref{fdip-009J}.}
